    {
      "id": "cand-a1-rd-0005",
      "turkish": "hâlâ",
      "english": "still",
      "priority": 3,
      "tags": [
        "adverb",
        "unit-a1-4c",
        "unit-a1"
      ],
      "source": "A1-4/5 reading exercise SÖZLÜK (Turkishle web)",
      "notes": "Both vowels long, and the l is the dark [ɫ] it becomes next to back vowels. Against hala (paternal aunt), ˈhɑ.ɫɑ — same shape, short, stressed on the first syllable. Convention: IPA, with ˈ before the stressed syllable, ː for length, [æ] vs [e] for the two e qualities, [c]/[ɟ] for palatalised k/g, and [l] vs [ɫ] for clear and dark l. Transcribed by hand — TDK supplies length, stress and a prose note on l, but not the vowel allophony or the k/g palatalisation, so this cannot be generated. Transcription removed. It was produced by applying my own rules — \"closed syllable gives æ\", \"ğ lengthens\" — rather than from evidence, and checking against en. and tr.wiktionary showed both rules over-generate: æ is conditioned by a following sonorant (ders, benzer) and not by a closed syllable as such, so any e before k, t, p or ş was wrong; and ğ lengthens only before a consonant, not between vowels. Left empty rather than guessed. Audio is in resources/pron_audio/ and scripts/check_pron.py looks the word up, so this can be restored from a source.",
      "status": "approved"
    },
